    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69505-x",
    "abstract": "Protected areas (PAs) are central to China's forest conservation strategy, yet their effectiveness for carbon storage across governance and management contexts remains unclear. A clearer understanding of their current and future carbon benefits is essential for informing conservation and climate policy. Here, using 1-km GEDI satellite data, we show that forests within China's PAs store on average 68.29 \u00b1 0.17 Mg C ha\u207b\u00b9 - about 13% more than matched unprotected forests. Carbon gains are highest in national parks (18.19 \u00b1 0.69 Mg C ha\u207b\u00b9) and in managed naturally regenerating forests (9.85 \u00b1 0.36 Mg C ha\u207b\u00b9), although some PA categories underperform. To assess future potential, we integrate GEDI observations with CMIP6 climate projections and find that under the high-emission SSP5-8.5 climate scenario, strongly protected areas could retain an additional ~600 \u00b1 36.39 Tg C by 2100. These results show that strong protection and optimized management substantially enhance China's carbon sink, offering major opportunities for climate mitigation and biodiversity conservation.",
    "field": "Forest carbon sequestration",
    "problem": "The effectiveness of China's protected areas for forest carbon storage remains unclear across diverse governance and management contexts, making it difficult to assess their current and future climate mitigation benefits and inform conservation policy.",
    "method": "This study combines GEDI satellite data with propensity score matching to assess forest carbon storage in protected areas and integrates these results with LPJ-GUESS model simulations to project future carbon gains under different climate scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the conservation effectiveness of China's protected areas for forest carbon storage and to project their future carbon sequestration potential under different climate scenarios and protection levels.",
    "model": [
        "CMIP6"
    ],
